1 big thing: A certain kind of AI investors&#x27; faith
 
 By Emily Peck 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios


 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Stocks are the new bonds in AI — Anthropic and Meta have turned to equity investors in looking for billions of dollars.

 Why it matters: After plowing billions into AI companies, bond investors have lately grown skeptical of funding open-ended asks for money. 

 Stock market investors, however, are all about throwing money at hopes and dreams even when the future payout may be hard to see. The big picture: The AI buildout requires a steady stream of investments and investors to stick with ambitious spending plans. 

 To get that cash, companies are pulling every available lever: stocks, private loans and project financing, which has soared this year , as well as bonds. "This was always going to need to be a bit of an all-hands-on-deck situation," Amanda Lynam, chief credit strategist at Goldman Sachs, tells Axios. Catch up quick: Three developments just yesterday illustrate that scramble:

 Meta is planning to raise up to $95 billion in stock sales — Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom might buy up to $5 billion of those shares, the company said. SpaceX secured a roughly $40 billion financing commitment from Apollo to lease AI chips and related equipment. And, of course, Anthropic filed to go public, setting the stage for possibly raising as much as $100 billion in the stock market. Where it stands: It all seemed to rekindle investors' zest for AI. The tech-heavy Nasdaq closed up for the day. And some AI stocks popped.

 "Many of the AI jitters are melting away," said Steve Sosnick, chief strategist at Interactive Brokers. Follow the money: That enthusiasm for all things AI in the wake of Anthropic should help sales of shares to the kinds of investors who "either believe the company is a big winner in a brave new world, or they don't," in the words of a Financial Times Lex column entitled "Why you shouldn't bother reading the Anthropic IPO filing."

 Zoom in: In contrast, the open-ended promise of the AI buildout counts against these companies when it comes to the credit markets.

 Bond investors like a borrowing binge with a finish line, says Goldman's Lynam. Think of a big M&amp;A deal where investors will know how much a company needs, when they'll need it and have a timeframe in mind for when they'll start paying down debt. AI isn't like that. Instead, investors are being asked to keep funding the same theme over years. Successive offerings need more incentives to attract buyers — and those sweeter deals can weigh on the value of bonds investors already own. Zoom out: In a recent note, Lynam wrote that there had been a correction in the debt markets around AI — investors are now asking for higher returns if they're lending to companies with riskier credit profiles, as we've written .

 Higher interest rates and weak trading in two huge September bond deals— from Paramount Skydance and SoftBank — also made investors more cautious, Lynam said. "It weighed on the risk tone." Yes, but: Stock investments in AI are not immune from investors' suddenly turning cautious. There's a lot that still has to go right for the AI trade to pay off, cautioned Sosnick of Interactive Brokers.

 "I don't want to say precarious, but if you step back, you know that it requires it requires a huge amount of, I don't know, faith," he said. The bottom line: AI companies are testing the limits of that faith. 


 
 
